\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Exact boundary modes}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{For the \vocabk{slab operator}{fdip-01AE}, write on
layer #{l}, using that layer's transverse shifts #{T_j^U},
##{\begin{aligned}
W_l&=\sum_{j\ne s}\left(\bbI -
   \tfrac12(T_j^U+(T_j^U)^\dagger)\right),\\
D_l&=\tfrac12\sum_{j\ne s} E_j(T_j^U-(T_j^U)^\dagger).
\end{aligned}}
The null fields supported on the bottom layer have exactly
the values
##{\im P_- \cap \ker D_1 \cap
\ker\bigl(W_1+(1-aM)\bbI\bigr).}
At the top the formula uses #{P_+,D_L,W_L}.}

\p{On a face with trivial transverse links, these values form
the orthogonal sum of the \vocabk{translation blocks}{fdip-016C}
at transverse corners with #{k} components #{\pi} and
#{aM=2k+1}, restricted to the indicated projector range.
If every transverse link is trivial and the normal link
#{U_s(x,l)} is independent of #{x} at every step, the two
single-face spaces exhaust #{\ker H_L(U)}. More generally, if
transverse shifts commute and normal transports intertwine them
between layers, the kernel consists of the two end ranges on
joint sign tuples with #{k} minus signs and #{aM=2k+1}.}

\proof{
\p{#{\ker H_L(U)=\ker K_{L,U}} since #{\Gamma} is invertible. In
#{aK_{L,U}} a bottom value #{v} contributes #{-P_+V_U^\dagger v}
on layer #{2}. Unitary transport commutes with #{P_+}, so this
vanishes exactly when #{P_+v=0}.
On the bottom layer the remaining two terms are
#{(W_1+(1-aM)\bbI)v} in #{\im P_-} and
#{D_1v} in #{\im P_+}. They vanish together exactly when each
vanishes. At the top the incoming hop tests #{P_-v}, giving
the other formula.}

\p{For a face with trivial transverse links write
#{p_j=2\pi r_j/N_j} and
##{\begin{aligned}
d(p)&=1-aM+\sum_{j\ne s}(1-\cos p_j),\\
b(p)&=\sum_{j\ne s} E_j\sin p_j,\qquad
c(p)=\sum_{j\ne s}\sin^2p_j.
\end{aligned}}
On this character the on-site operator is #{d(p)\bbI+\ui b(p)}.
Its adjoint times itself is #{(d(p)^2+c(p))\bbI}, since
#{b(p)} is self-adjoint and its Clifford square is #{c(p)\bbI}.
The finite geometric sum in \ref{fdip-000S} includes periods one
and two. Hence only sampled corners with #{d(p)=0} contribute. In dimension #{2} at #{aM=1}, only #{p=0} survives:
the bottom and top fields are constant, with values in
#{\im P_-} and #{\im P_+}, respectively.}

\p{For trivial transverse links take layerwise gauges #{g_1=\bbI_F}, #{g_{l+1}=g_lU_s(l)^{-1}}. These [gauges](fdip-009O) set the normal links to the identity
and preserve the transverse links and the projector ranges. With #{V} the forward shift in this gauge,
put #{N=P_-V+P_+V^\dagger}. Each shift leaves the open chain after #{L} steps. Thus
#{V^L=(V^\dagger)^L=0} and, on the respective normal ranges, #{N^L=0}. Each character has #{aK_{L,U}=(d(p)\bbI -N)+\ui b(p)}.
Since #{b(p)} exchanges the normal ranges and commutes with #{V},
#{N^\dagger b(p)=b(p)N}. Consequently
##{a^2H_L(U)^2=(d(p)\bbI -N)^\dagger(d(p)\bbI -N)+c(p)\bbI .}
A noncorner has #{c(p)>0}. At a corner with #{d(p)\ne0},
multiplying #{d(p)\bbI -N} by
#{\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}d(p)^{L-1-j}N^j} gives #{d(p)^L\bbI}.
At a matching corner the two components of #{N} kill only
the bottom #{P_-} field and the top #{P_+} field, respectively.
The gauges preserve each of these single-face spaces.}

\p{For commuting, intertwined shifts use the same gauges pointwise
in #{x}. They identify the transverse shifts on every layer.
The grading and Clifford actions preserve their joint subspaces.
On each, insert its scalar multipliers #{\ue^{\ui p_j}} in the same
square formula. It gives exactly the stated end spaces.}
}
